[00:00] [scene:intro]
VISUAL: A button with `hx-post` and `hx-target` attributes is shown, demonstrating a like counter updating without JavaScript.
AUDIO: 'This is a button. You click it and the page updates. Fresh data straight from the server, no reload. Now, here\'s the strange part. There\'s no JavaScript here. None that you wrote. The whole thing is one HTML attribute. To do that same little update the modern way, you\'d'

[00:13] [scene:ui]
VISUAL: A diagram illustrates HTML making a request to a server, which returns a chunk of plain HTML.
AUDIO: 'reach for React, a component, a piece of state, a fetch call, a JSON endpoint on the server, and a re-render to stitch it all back together. That is a lot of moving parts for one number on a screen.'

[00:25] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'HTMX throws all of that out. The secret is almost insultingly simple. Let plain HTML talk to the server directly and just swap in whatever HTML comes back. Dumb, and it works. This is HTMX, about'

[00:38] [scene:ui]
VISUAL: A flowchart shows the HTMX loop: `hx-get` on an element, HTMX fires a request, server sends HTML.
AUDIO: '16 kilobytes, zero dependencies, written in plain JavaScript you never have to touch. Made by Carson Gross, a computer science professor in Montana, not a trillion-dollar framework team. Here\'s'

[00:50] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'the entire idea. You add hx-get to an element, click it, and HTMX fires an HTTP request for you. The server sends back a chunk of HTML. HTMX drops that HTML right into the page. Request,'

[01:03] [scene:ui]
VISUAL: Examples of HTMX attributes on `div`, `tr`, and `input` elements are displayed, showing "any element, any event" functionality.
AUDIO: 'response, swap. That\'s the whole loop. And it\'s not just buttons. In HTMX, any element can make a request. A div, a table row, an input that searches as you type. And the thing that triggers it'

[01:16] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'doesn\'t have to be a click. It can be any event at all. It speaks the full web, too. Get, post, put, delete, every HTTP verb, not just the two that HTML forms gave you. And you choose exactly'

[01:28] [scene:comparison]
VISUAL: A bar chart compares the size of HTMX (~16KB) to React + DOM (~45KB) and a real SPA (300KB+).
AUDIO: 'which part of the page gets replaced. Any element, any event, any verb, any target. Now, about that size. HTMX is around 16 kilobytes. React plus React DOM, just the core, before your app even'

[01:42] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'starts, is roughly 45. And a real single-page app ships hundreds of kilobytes of JavaScript before it shows you a thing. And this is no toy. One real company, Contexty, rewrote their React app in HTMX. 67% less code. Their'

[01:56] [scene:stats]
VISUAL: A GitHub star count of 39k for HTMX is shown, indicating its popularity.
AUDIO: 'JavaScript dependencies dropped from 255 down to nine, and build times fell by nearly 90%. So, it\'s no surprise HTMX blew up. 48,000 stars on GitHub, the'

[02:08] [scene:ui]
VISUAL: An example of a button with `hx-post`, `hx-target`, and `hx-swap` attributes is detailed, explaining their functions.
AUDIO: 'number one rising star in front end, and in the big developer surveys, one of the single most admired tools in the entire industry. Let\'s slow down and actually read one. HX-post says where to send the'

[02:20] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'request. HX-target says which element on the page should change. And HX-swap says how. Replace the insides, the whole element, or just append to it. Three attributes. That\'s a'

[02:32] [scene:ui]
VISUAL: A UI demonstrating a delete action with `hx-delete` is shown, removing a row from a list.
AUDIO: 'feature. HX-trigger decides when it fires. A click, a form change, a key press, debounce, so it waits until you stop typing. Even on a timer, every couple of seconds for a live dashboard. It\'s the event'

[02:45] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'model HTML always wanted, because it speaks every verb your interface maps cleanly onto real actions. A delete button sends an actual HTTP delete. The server removes the record, returns'

[02:57] [scene:ui]
VISUAL: A live search input with `hx-get` on `keyup` is shown, dynamically updating results.
AUDIO: 'nothing, and HTMX swaps that row right out of existence. No state to reconcile. This is the demo that sold a whole generation. An input with HDX-get firing on keyup. You type, HTMX queries the'

[03:09] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'server, the server returns table rows as HTML, and they swap straight into the results. Live search in about three lines with no API to build. And notice what the server sends back. Not JSON,'

[03:22] [scene:ui]
VISUAL: A shopping cart UI demonstrates an "out of band swap" updating both the cart contents and a cart badge.
AUDIO: 'not raw data for you to assemble, just HTML, finished, ready to display. Which means your back end can be literally anything. Python, Ruby, Go, PHP, Node. If it can return HTML, it speaks HTMX.'

[03:36] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'It can even update two places at once. An out of band swap lets a single response patch the thing you clicked, and say the cart count up in the corner. One request, two updates, zero glue code, and HX-boost quietly upgrades'

[03:50] [scene:ui]
VISUAL: A navigation bar with `hx-boost="true"` is shown, illustrating a single-page feel with plain HTML links.
AUDIO: 'ordinary links and forms into background requests, swapping just the body instead of reloading. You get that smooth, no-flash, single-page feel on a site that\'s really just plain HTML pages'

[04:02] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'underneath. It even handles the unglamorous parts. A loading spinner while a request is in flight, that\'s one attribute. The states you usually hand-wire in JavaScript are just built in. Underneath all of this is an old'

[04:14] [scene:ui]
VISUAL: An HTML snippet shows how the server response carries the state and next actions (hypermedia).
AUDIO: 'idea with a fancy name, hypermedia. The server doesn\'t just send data. It sends the next set of actions baked right into the HTML. The page itself carries the state of your application. It\'s how the'

[04:26] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'web was designed to work. And here\'s the part that breaks people\'s brains. This is REST, the real REST, the way Roy Fielding actually defined it. Somewhere along the line, the word REST got hijacked to mean a JSON API. HTMX is'

[04:39] [scene:comparison]
VISUAL: A comparison shows React requiring multiple files for an action versus HTMX achieving it in one line of HTML.
AUDIO: 'just quietly going back. It also brings back a quality we quietly lost, locality of behavior. Look at the element, and you can see exactly what it does right there in the markup. No hunting through'

[04:52] [scene:text]
VISUAL: The title of the book "Hypermedia Systems" is displayed.
AUDIO: 'a dozen files to trace one little click. Think about what disappears. In the React world, you keep state twice, once on the server, once in the browser, and write an API forever just to sync them.'

[05:04] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'HTMX keeps a single copy on the server, and sends HTML. A whole category of bugs simply evaporates. There\'s a whole culture around this, too. A free book, Hypermedia Systems, lays out the entire'

[05:16] [scene:ui]
VISUAL: A grid of backend frameworks (Django, Flask, FastAPI, Rails, Laravel, Go, Express) is shown, representing the HOWL stack.
AUDIO: 'philosophy. And the HTMX account online has become legendary, equal parts serious engineering and deadpan jokes about how everyone somehow is the CEO of HTMX.'

[05:29] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'Because it\'s back-end agnostic, the community even has a name for the stack, HOWL, Hypermedia on whatever you\'d like. Django, Flask, FastAPI, Rails, Laravel, Go, Express. Pick your language. HTMX'

[05:42] [scene:ui]
VISUAL: Alpine.js and Hyperscript are presented as "tiny partners for the client."
AUDIO: 'genuinely does not care. And when you do need real client-side interactivity, a drop-down, a toggle, a little animation, you reach for a tiny partner like Alpine.js or Hyperscript. Small tools'

[05:54] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'sprinkled in where you need them instead of one giant framework running everything. If this is reminding you of Rails Hotwire and Turbo, you\'re right. Same instinct, different flavor. Turbo'

[06:06] [scene:caveats]
VISUAL: Three scenarios where HTMX is not suitable are listed: Spreadsheets, Drag-maps, Offline apps.
AUDIO: 'is more opinionated and batteries included. HTMX is lower level and does only what you explicitly tell it to on any back-end you like. Now, let\'s be honest because HTMX fans often aren\'t.'

[06:18] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'This is the wrong tool for some apps. A spreadsheet like Google Sheets, a map you drag in real time, anything offline or with thousands of interdependent widgets reacting instantly. That is'

[06:30] [scene:comparison]
VISUAL: A comparison highlights React for "deeply interactive apps" and HTMX for "90% of the rest of the web."
AUDIO: 'React\'s home turf and it isn\'t close. So, no, this isn\'t React\'s funeral. If you\'re building a deeply interactive client application, reach for React. That\'s exactly what it\'s brilliant at. HTMX is for the other 90% of the web,'

[06:43] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'forms, dashboards, content, CRUD. The boring stuff that\'s secretly most of everything. The team now calls the 2.x line feature complete and HTMX 4 is already in beta, folding in DOM morphing'

[06:57] [scene:conclusion]
VISUAL: A statement "Maybe the website was the framework all along" is displayed.
AUDIO: 'and streaming responses. But, the pitch hasn\'t changed since day one. Do more by writing far less. So, is dumb HTML really replacing complex React apps? For a huge slice of the web, yes, and'

[07:10] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'developers sound genuinely relieved. The radical idea HTMX is quietly selling is just this. Maybe the website was the framework all along. So, to recap, HTMX is about 16 kilobytes that let HTML talk'

[07:23] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'to the server. It swaps in HTML instead of juggling JSON. It revives real hypermedia and real REST. And for most apps, it deletes a staggering amount of complexity.'

